{"version":1,"ops":[{"type":1,"author":{"id":"607ac2a9afabc0e44debe80e34283e413e009770"},"timestamp":1525804449,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDU6SXNzdWUzMjEyOTg1NDc=","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/2481","origin":"github"},"title":"Default result format is inaccessible via 'datalad -f default ...'","message":"If a command sets a custom `result_renderer`, I don't see a way to get the non-custom format. Looking at `datalad --help`, I'd expect `-f default` to always give me this format.\n\n    [...]\n      -f {default,json,json_pp,tailored,'\u003ctemplate\u003e'}, --output-format {default,json,json_pp,tailored,'\u003ctemplate\u003e'}\n                            select format for returned command results. 'default'\n                            give one line per result reporting action, status,\n                            path and an optional message;\n    [...]\n\nHowever, \"default\" instead uses whatever is specified by the class's `result_renderer`.\n\nThis is because we ignore `common_output_format` if it is \"default\":\n\n\u003chttps://github.com/datalad/datalad/blob/43e37edc6f00b6edf250f1edc0116b9bd62f3f87/datalad/interface/base.py#L403-L405\u003e\n\nSo I'd say that the problem is that we're conflating the default renderer for a command and the \"default\" output format.  Perhaps we should give the output format a name other than \"default\", so that `-f default` means \"use the command's default renderer\" and `-f \u003cother name\u003e` means \"use the format described in the help message above\".\n\nThoughts?\n\n---\n\nHere's an example of what I'm talking about.\n\n```python\nfrom datalad.interface.base import Interface\nfrom datalad.interface.base import build_doc\nfrom datalad.interface.results import get_status_dict\n\n\n@build_doc\nclass Foo(Interface):\n    from datalad.distribution.dataset import EnsureDataset\n    from datalad.distribution.dataset import datasetmethod\n    from datalad.interface.utils import eval_results\n    from datalad.support.constraints import EnsureNone\n    from datalad.support.param import Parameter\n\n    result_renderer = \"json_pp\"\n\n    _params_ = dict(\n        dataset=Parameter(\n            args=(\"-d\", \"--dataset\"),\n            doc=\"\"\"\"dataset to foo.\"\"\",\n            constraints=EnsureDataset() | EnsureNone()),\n    )\n\n    @staticmethod\n    @eval_results\n    def __call__(dataset=None):\n        yield get_status_dict(\"foo\",\n                              message=\"did foo\",\n                              type=\"dataset\",\n                              path=dataset.path if dataset else \"nowhere\",\n                              status=\"ok\")\n\n\n__datalad_plugin__ = Foo\n```\n\n```shell\n% datalad foo\n{\n  \"action\": \"foo\",\n  \"path\": \"nowhere\",\n  \"status\": \"ok\",\n  \"type\": \"dataset\"\n}\n\n% datalad -f json foo\n{\"action\": \"foo\", \"path\": \"nowhere\", \"status\": \"ok\", \"type\": \"dataset\"}\n\n% datalad -f default foo\n{\n  \"action\": \"foo\",\n  \"path\": \"nowhere\",\n  \"status\": \"ok\",\n  \"type\": \"dataset\"\n}\n```","files":null}]}